The INV-rozaj fix (PR #200) made _qualified_callee normalise an in-repo
callee's module slot with _module_from_symbol_path, which strips the source
file extension. That is correct - it is what makes a first-party callee
catalogueable at all - but the extension had also been an ACCIDENTAL barrier:

    before:  go:net/http.go:1-5:Get:function -> 'net/http.go.Get'  != stdlib
    after:   go:net/http.go:1-5:Get:function -> 'net/http.Get'     == stdlib

A Go repo with a root-level net/http.go therefore produces a key equal to a
SHIPPED stdlib summary. This is the WI-damir shape - a first-party symbol
matching a catalogue primitive by name alone - reappearing in the section 4
lookup rather than in sink matching. WI-damir already settled the premise:
resolution establishes WHICH IN-REPO SYMBOL is called and says nothing about
whether that symbol IS the catalogued primitive.

The gate is deliberately NOT in _qualified_callee. Gating key CONSTRUCTION
would break test_in_repo_callee_key_has_no_file_extension, which pins the
INV-rozaj fix. The key shape was never the defect; handing a first-party key
to a shipped catalogue was. So the provenance decision gets its own home,
_catalogue_key_for_edge, and the key builder is untouched.

The verdict is read from Edge.is_resolved, never from the dst suffix
(ADR-0037 ruling 4): WI-pubiv's remap rewrites ':unresolved' to
':external_symbol' on the final graph, so a string check would read every
unresolved edge as resolved and bypass the gate. Pinned by its own test.

DIRECTION. A summary that says 'terminates' lets the section 3a walk CLOSE a
branch, and a false close REMOVES a real finding. Refusing to catalogue
produces strictly fewer terminations, hence more surviving violations. The
.get('is_resolved', True) default carries the same direction - an edge with no
resolution verdict is treated as first-party and is not catalogued.

MEASURED EFFECT: ZERO, with a positive control so the zero is readable. Across
55,779 taint call edges on caddy + pretix, 2,668 matched a shipped qualified
key before the gate and 2,668 after. The control is a synthetic resolved edge
whose key is deliberately a real shipped key (Array.prototype.filter); it IS
refused, so the zero is absence rather than a broken predicate. The filing had
sized this only on a self-survey and never on the cohort.

Two claims in the item's own shipped red test are corrected in place. It
predicted 'if a guard lands, this should FAIL' - it did not, and should not
have. And its urgency argument rested on 'nothing acts on False', which has
been untrue since PR #214: the same-function sanitizer path runs a second walk
whose False earns 'sanitized' and drops the flow from a claim's violation set.

